1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,539
Decibels exist because radio numbers are
ridiculous.

2
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:13,677
The signal your antenna picks up from a repeater ten miles away can be a hundred
billion times weaker than the signal leaving that repeater's transmitter.

3
00:00:13,597 --> 00:00:16,660
Nobody wants to do math with eleven zeros.

4
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:21,722
The decibel is how radio engineers make
those numbers small, friendly — and addable.

5
00:00:21,872 --> 00:00:26,804
A decibel is a logarithmic way of writing
a ratio between two power levels.

6
00:00:26,724 --> 00:00:28,835
Not an amount — a ratio.

7
00:00:28,755 --> 00:00:31,633
Ten decibels means ten times the power.

8
00:00:31,553 --> 00:00:34,233
Twenty decibels means a hundred times.

9
00:00:34,153 --> 00:00:35,823
Thirty means a thousand.

10
00:00:35,743 --> 00:00:39,526
Every ten decibels you add multiplies the
power by another ten.

11
00:00:39,676 --> 00:00:41,613
The everyday anchor is a piano.

12
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:46,790
Each octave is the same-sized step to your
ear, but each one doubles the frequency.

13
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:51,608
Decibels do that for power: equal steps
that stand for equal multiplications.

14
00:00:51,528 --> 00:00:57,935
Your ear and your radio both live on that kind of scale,
which is why decibels feel natural once they click.

15
00:00:58,085 --> 00:01:00,440
Three numbers are worth memorizing.

16
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,923
Plus three decibels: double the power.

17
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:04,699
Minus three: half.

18
00:01:04,619 --> 00:01:06,719
Plus ten: ten times.

19
00:01:06,639 --> 00:01:09,679
That's it — every other value is a
combination.

20
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:11,199
Sixteen decibels?

21
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:16,887
That's ten plus three plus three: ten times
two times two — forty times the power.

22
00:01:16,807 --> 00:01:19,812
You just did logarithms in your head
without noticing.

23
00:01:19,962 --> 00:01:24,906
And here's the superpower: on a log scale,
multiplication becomes addition.

24
00:01:24,826 --> 00:01:35,250
Follow a signal from transmitter to receiver — every cable loses a few decibels,
every antenna adds a few, the path between them takes away a hundred or more.

25
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:41,345
Instead of multiplying eleven-digit numbers, you
just add and subtract small ones down the chain.

26
00:01:41,265 --> 00:01:46,778
That running sum is called a link budget, and
it's how every radio link on Earth is designed.

27
00:01:46,928 --> 00:01:47,983
One trap.

28
00:01:47,903 --> 00:01:52,812
A plain decibel is always relative — a
ratio between two things.

29
00:01:52,732 --> 00:02:07,312
The moment you see a letter after it, it's become an absolute number, measured against a fixed reference:
d-B-m means compared to one milliwatt; d-B-F-S means compared to the loudest sample your SDR can represent.

30
00:02:07,232 --> 00:02:09,239
Same math, different anchor.

31
00:02:09,389 --> 00:02:21,275
You'll see all three flavors in GopherTrunk's dashboard: signal level in d-B-F-S,
signal-to-noise ratio in plain decibels, and hardware specs quoted in d-B-m.

32
00:02:21,195 --> 00:02:25,176
Now they're not three mysteries — they're
one idea with three anchors.

33
00:02:26,126 --> 00:02:35,772
So: decibels turn absurd ratios into small numbers, multiplication
into addition, and three memorized steps into fluent mental math.

34
00:02:35,692 --> 00:02:39,173
